    <paragraph>Simplify:</paragraph>
    <list type="ordered">
      <item>ⓐ $$k^{0}$$</item>
      <item>ⓑ $$29^{0}$$</item>
    </list>
  </note>
  <paragraph>Now that we have defined the zero exponent, we can expand all the Properties of Exponents to include whole number exponents.</paragraph>
  <paragraph>What about raising an expression to the zero power? Let's look at $$\left( {2x} \right)^{0}.$$ We can use the product to a power rule to rewrite this expression.</paragraph>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell>$$\left( {2x} \right)^{0}$$</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Use the Product to a Power Rule.</cell>
        <cell>$$2^{0}x^{0}$$</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Use the Zero Exponent Property.</cell>
        <cell>$$1 \cdot 1$$</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Simplify.</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <paragraph>This tells us that any non-zero expression raised to the zero power is one.</paragraph>
  <example number="10.49">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify: $$\left( {7z} \right)^{0}.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
